WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.889 --> 00:00:03.889
Hello everybody.

00:00:03.889 --> 00:00:05.290
This is Peter Cooper from the NCBI.

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:07.950
Welcome to today's webinar.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:11.219
This is a A bBeginner's Guide to Genes and
Sequences at NCBI.

00:00:11.219 --> 00:00:17.460
Before we begin, I want to point out something
about this particular talk.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:24.570
This was prompted by some librarians at Fanshawe
college in Canada who asked us to present

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:28.609
something to them about how to use NCBI to
find sequences.

00:00:28.609 --> 00:00:33.800
In particular, it was prompted I think by
some undergraduate questions that were asked

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.510
in a course to find certain things.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:40.600
I thought it would be useful for a lot of
people.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:44.199
There are a lot of holes in people's knowledge
about how NCBI works and I thought it would

00:00:44.199 --> 00:00:46.940
be helpful for everybody to go through this.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.559
Let's hope we can do this today.

00:00:48.559 --> 00:00:51.730
I hope to spend most of the time doing some
live demos.

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:54.859
I will show you a few slides to start out
with.

00:00:54.859 --> 00:00:59.870
We will talk a little bit about genes and
sequences and how we represent them at NCBI.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:03.910
We will talk about some sequence data and
where they are from.

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:09.250
And we will point out that the traditional
search systems and tools, in particular the

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:16.320
Entrez system , our traditional text search
system, and BLAST, which is the other way

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.659
of searching sequences at NCBI, that is using
sequence similarity.

00:01:20.659 --> 00:01:27.470
We have a new search experience, I guess you
would call it that is happening now all over

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:28.509
the NCBI site.

00:01:28.509 --> 00:01:35.549
If you type certain things into the search
box , you will activate the results, sort

00:01:35.549 --> 00:01:36.549
of de-silod.

00:01:36.549 --> 00:01:38.450
You don't know have to know which database
to search, it will only give you results that

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:39.450
are relevant.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:42.780
We'll use that to do the things I want to
do today.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.899
I will show you some of those things in a
live demonstration.

00:01:45.899 --> 00:01:51.220
Okay, so what are we trying to represent at
NCBI?

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:55.399
This is a diagram that is stolen from NHGRI.

00:01:55.399 --> 00:02:04.900
We are trying to represent the central dogma
of molecular biology, I think Jim Watson called

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:05.900
it that.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:12.220
Basically, you have DNA that contains genes
and it is expressed by being transcribed into

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:13.540
RNA.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:18.370
Those RNA sequences in higher eukaryotes make
multiple kinds of products through alternative

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:19.370
splicing.

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:27.840
And, those products, those spliced mRNAs are
translated into proteins that are folded up

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:32.120
to do their business of the cell and life
in general.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:34.510
So, we want to try to represent that information
at NCBI.

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:39.250
And, those are the things we will try to pull
out today for a particular gene.

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:44.069
So, how do we represent that particular dogma
at NCBI?

00:02:44.069 --> 00:02:48.590
This is a screenshot from our genome browser.

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:52.800
This is the gene I am going to talk about
today, the APRTgene.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:59.370
It has two transcript variants represented
there and each transcript produces its own

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:00.410
protein isoforms.

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:06.989
They are represented at NCBI by these sequence
records that have various kinds of accession

00:03:06.989 --> 00:03:10.409
numbers we call them, and they are listed
in the box at the bottom.

00:03:10.409 --> 00:03:13.780
I will come back to that in a minute and show
you all of the different kinds of sequences

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:16.460
we have in this particular gene.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:22.790
So, lets talk about where sequences come from.

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:28.121
Many people when they think about sequences
at NCBI often thnk of the word GenBank, which

00:03:28.121 --> 00:03:31.239
refers to a particular set of submitted sequences.

00:03:31.239 --> 00:03:38.870
GenBank and two other DNA databases, the European
Nucleotide Archive and the DNA Databank of

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:41.000
Japan are sequence database archives.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:46.510
They contain submitted sequences and are part
of the International Sequence Database Collaboration

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:47.510
(INSDC).

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:50.900
These are sequences that are provided to us,
we don't own them, we don't control them,

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:51.900
we don't edit them.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:58.410
They go directly into this resource called
the INSDC or GenBank; you can download it.

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:03.230
IT is mixed in with all of our stuff on the
web.

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:08.459
There are other sequences at NCBI and those
are the ones we're going to focus on today.

00:04:08.459 --> 00:04:11.590
The main one being the NCBI Reference sequences.

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:17.049
These are sequences that we generate from
the submitted sequences to represent particular

00:04:17.049 --> 00:04:23.090
genes and gene products, proteins and things
like that.

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:27.730
They are are less redundant set with more
curation.

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:32.110
There are other curated sequences at NCBI,
Swiss-Prot and another interesting set of

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:36.150
sequences that come from structures and we'll
use those today to look at a structure, because

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:40.441
we want to look at the whole paradigm to get
down to the folded up protein, we'll look

00:04:40.441 --> 00:04:45.660
at the structure of the ARPT protein today.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:51.560
This is a very busy slide with lots of identifiers
on it.

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:52.560
I apologize for that.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:57.390
To show you the different kinds of sequences
we have for the APRT gene and it's products.

00:04:57.390 --> 00:05:02.450
The main one we're going to focus on is the
box in the upper lEST hand corner where it

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:07.500
has the transcripts and the proteins, and
the genomic sequences, the primary assembly,

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:13.000
it's a chromosome 16 record, and a RefSeqGene
record, which is a stand alone record designed

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:14.770
to represent that gene.

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:20.110
As you can see there are lots of other sequences,
these are just representatives.

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:25.130
There are sequences in GenBank for the transcripts
and genomic parts of this gene.

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:29.710
And there are protein-only records from other
databases, like PDB.

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:32.690
That's how we can connect to the structure
record.

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:38.180
And there is also a Swiss-Prot, or UniProtKB
for this protein.

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:45.120
And just to get back to this PDB idea, this
is what we'll do with PDB sequences today.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:46.470
We have a RefSeq.

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:51.620
Through BLAST similarity we know it is, in
this case, almost identical to a protein chain

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:55.880
in this PDB record, so we can follow that
link to get a structure for the protein chain.

00:05:55.880 --> 00:06:00.570
We'll do that as an example in just a few
minutes.

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:07.050
On the web this is all represented largely
in two databases, the Nucleotide database

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:11.250
the the Protein database and you can search
these separately, which is a problem that

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:13.320
we'll try to resolve today.

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:15.810
There are other sequences, of course, at NCBI.

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:19.160
The next-gen data, the SRA, is not part of
this talk.

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:24.570
How do we search this stuff?

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:30.440
Traditionally, we use two search systems,
the Entrez text system, which you can do from

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:33.370
any pull down list on the NCBI pages.

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:38.740
And the BLAST sequence similarity search system.

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:43.030
The first one you search with terms, the second
one you search with sequences.

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:46.540
The Entrez system has 40 databases.

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:53.250
To use it effectively you often need to use
a formal syntax fielded queries and things

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:55.610
like that.

00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:58.490
Because of that it is difficult to decide
where to start.

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:01.960
Traditionally what I've told people to do
is start in a central hub database like Gene

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:03.910
where you are connected to all the different
things.

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:08.830
In fact, we'll leverage Gene today when we
try the new search experience.

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:14.080
Likewise, BLAST, you start out with a sequence
and you ask for related sequences.

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:19.471
The BLAST databases are getting quite large
and this is sometimes a daunting task to find

00:07:19.471 --> 00:07:20.900
the sequences you want.

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:26.940
So the new search experience is available
now and it does make a lot of this easier.

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:34.030
If you type certain things into a search box
at NCBI, you'll get this compact set of results

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:36.900
that are relevant to the thing you are looking
for.

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:40.800
We can tell from your query what you are looking
for but often the results from these database

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:44.160
searches often don't make a lot of sense in
terms of that.

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:48.500
This is an effort to make the interface a
little more Google-like, where it is independent

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:52.870
of which silo or database the data are in.

00:07:52.870 --> 00:08:00.120
So for example we're going to find the ARPT
sequences by searching with either the gene

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:03.510
name or the name of the protein.

00:08:03.510 --> 00:08:10.870
You get a result that shows you the gene,
the sequences, and you have access to related

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:12.840
sequences there through the orthologs button.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:21.680
The Orthologs button is a partial replacement
for BLAST because you can quickly find the

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:28.430
related proteins and related sequences through
that system without doing a BLAST search.

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:33.930
And you could even launch a sequence comparison
from here and we will do that today.

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:38.300
We will also play around a little bit with
blast, because that is one of the questions

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:41.290
the library asked us.

00:08:41.290 --> 00:08:50.130
We will get some sequences for this enzyme
and its gene, adenine ribosyltransferase.

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:55.890
And, we will use this known item search, the
thing where we know what you are looking for

00:08:55.890 --> 00:09:01.130
for finding related sequences, like orthologs,
similar genes, we will use our multiple sequence

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:05.810
alignment tool COBALT, we will download some
genes, we will compare some mRNA sequences

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:09.970
using BLAST 2 sequences, you can use more
than two sequences.

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:18.060
Finally, we will use blast in a traditional
way to find a bacterial transferase and we

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:22.740
will find the structure of that protein and
look at it in iCn3D.

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:33.510
What I want to do now is escape out of this
and go to a web browser.

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:37.290
The first thing we want to do is do a traditional
nucleotide search.

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:45.050
Notice, we have to pick the Nucleotide database
from here.

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:47.220
I will do a search, and I think I typed this
wrong.

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:49.390
I want to say adenine phosphoribosyltransferase.

00:09:49.390 --> 00:09:55.390
On the last slide I lEST off the phospho.

00:09:55.390 --> 00:10:02.190
So, I am going to type this, if I can.

00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:04.770
You think I would have picked one that wasn't
so long.

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:13.170
I could search with the gene symbol since
I know that one, although that would be a

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:19.200
spoiler, because that would give you something
you could see right away.

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:25.470
This is the traditional way people would try
to find sequences within NCBI.

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:30.410
Right away you are confronted with an aspect
of the new search experience.

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:34.830
I've typed what is essentially the name of
an enzyme or a protein and what I have been

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:40.050
given by the search is a bacterial protein
that has this name.

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:42.460
That is not really what we are after today.

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:47.250
We will come back in a minute and fix this
so it gives me what I want.

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:52.420
But just to show you, to get some basics for
this, we have a lot of sequences here.

00:10:52.420 --> 00:10:55.561
We have 154,000 from INSDC.

00:10:55.561 --> 00:11:01.380
162,000 from RefSeq so there is a lot to sort
through here.

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:06.280
Let's fix this in a simple way to get the
human refseqs for this.

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:08.380
So I'll click on this RefSeq filter.

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:12.780
I will do an old-fashioned Entrez query.

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:20.500
I will do a Boolean operator here, AND, and
I'll type human and I will do a fielded search,

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:24.210
[organism], this is a complex syntax.

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:25.780
That is a good one to know about.

00:11:25.780 --> 00:11:32.380
You can quickly get sequences for the organism
you want this way.

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:39.019
And boom, under there I have my reference
sequences, the transcripts, the RefSeqGene

00:11:39.019 --> 00:11:42.490
, the chromosome 16 record that has this gene
on it.

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:47.970
There are also some extra genes that are overlapping
on either side of this gene.

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:50.921
Now, I am in a silo.

00:11:50.921 --> 00:11:55.940
I have nucleotide sequences, but suppose I
want the protein sequences.

00:11:55.940 --> 00:11:59.640
I would have to go over to protein and do
the same thing.

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:03.310
So, things are segregated and it is not useful
in that way.

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:09.350
Let me show you something I didn't show you,
because I just wanted to talk a minute about

00:12:09.350 --> 00:12:12.630
the traditional search, because you still
need to use that sometimes.

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:20.980
But, if you type the right things in here,
you will activate the new search.

00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:25.420
Eventually, you won't have to know what those
right things are.

00:12:25.420 --> 00:12:28.440
This is the kind of thing that somebody might
search for,

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:35.670
human adenine phosphoribosyltransferase,
and lo and behold, I now have the sensor and

00:12:35.670 --> 00:12:41.670
the results for APRT for human.

00:12:41.670 --> 00:12:46.029
This link goes to the Gene record where I
can get all kinds of useful information about

00:12:46.029 --> 00:12:48.120
this gene and a little bit about the biology.

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:53.440
One of the reasons I picked this is because
it does have some diseases associated with

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:54.440
mutations in it.

00:12:54.440 --> 00:13:02.170
It is also a highly conserved function, involved
in purine metabolism.

00:13:02.170 --> 00:13:07.070
Every form of life has something that does
this.

00:13:07.070 --> 00:13:08.840
So, we can do a lot with this one.

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:12.870
It is actually a small gene, which makes it
easier to work with when we are looking at

00:13:12.870 --> 00:13:14.310
it.

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:17.790
Let's take a look a little bit about the genomic
context which we saw on a slide earlier.

00:13:17.790 --> 00:13:22.830
I can go to the genome browser and click that
button.

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:26.680
And we can see the same thing I showed you
in the slide a minute ago to show you how

00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:29.150
we represent this.

00:13:29.150 --> 00:13:34.100
This is a complex display, and I will clean
it up a little bit and get rid of things we

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:42.380
don't need to talk about today.

00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:45.560
There are the two transcripts shown here.

00:13:45.560 --> 00:13:49.730
And, I can expand this a little bit more if
I want to.

00:13:49.730 --> 00:13:54.670
This Show All for the gene track, that is
the track I am showing now, will give you

00:13:54.670 --> 00:13:57.100
the same thing we saw in my slide.

00:13:57.100 --> 00:14:04.160
Here are the transcripts and the proteins,
and you can even zoom in to the sequence level

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:10.850
if you want to to see the actual codons and
how they code for proteins.

00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:13.950
So let's zoom to sequence.

00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:16.589
And this gene is on the opposite strand so
it has to be read backwards.

00:14:16.589 --> 00:14:21.180
There are the codons and the amino acids in
the two protein isoforms.

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:26.089
That is one thing that is useful to do when
you're trying to look at genomic context for

00:14:26.089 --> 00:14:27.089
things.

00:14:27.089 --> 00:14:35.070
Now, let's go back over here and we will spend
some time thinking about the reference sequences

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:38.589
and how we can get related sequences.

00:14:38.589 --> 00:14:43.190
A couple things to point out about the transcripts,
notice there are two splice variants for this

00:14:43.190 --> 00:14:44.190
gene.

00:14:44.190 --> 00:14:49.050
We want to make a shout out to the RefSeq
Select project here.

00:14:49.050 --> 00:14:53.910
This is a way of picking a preliminary representative
transcript for this gene.

00:14:53.910 --> 00:14:55.490
This is the one we will work with.

00:14:55.490 --> 00:14:58.300
Many times, and I had this happen to me many
times.

00:14:58.300 --> 00:15:02.340
You don't want all of these splice variants,
you just want want ome to represent the gene.

00:15:02.340 --> 00:15:04.900
This is a handy feature and we will use this
today.

00:15:04.900 --> 00:15:11.250
Now, if we are tasked with finding related
sequences in other organisms, we could take

00:15:11.250 --> 00:15:14.970
this protein and do a BLAST search.

00:15:14.970 --> 00:15:21.029
You can find related sequences by going up
here to the Orthologs button.

00:15:21.029 --> 00:15:26.200
What that does is it gives you a set of vertebrate
orthologs.

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:29.700
These are coming from our genome annotation
pipeline.

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:33.029
You can quickly find all of those we have
found.

00:15:33.029 --> 00:15:35.610
This includes genomic context and other things.

00:15:35.610 --> 00:15:38.800
These are truly the corresponding genes in
those species.

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:42.110
You can filter this down to get the organism
you want.

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:49.310
So, if I want to find the channel catfish
gene, I can do that and get the information

00:15:49.310 --> 00:15:50.310
I had before.

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:54.340
There is the transcript and a protein right
here.

00:15:54.340 --> 00:15:57.020
I can add these to my cart and do things with
them.

00:15:57.020 --> 00:16:02.060
I am more interested in seeing a broader spectrum
of genes, more like what I would get with

00:16:02.060 --> 00:16:03.340
BLAST.

00:16:03.340 --> 00:16:08.690
So let me go over to this other link, Genes
similar to APRT.

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:15.920
Now, we have access to a much broader range
of things.

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:21.450
So, you can see we are going back to C. elegans.

00:16:21.450 --> 00:16:26.120
There is a Drosophila sequences, a yeast sequence.

00:16:26.120 --> 00:16:30.230
So, I can pick out some of these, for example
and do things with them.

00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:37.650
So let me do mouse, human and let's pick some
of the classic organisms.

00:16:37.650 --> 00:16:48.020
Drosophila, Yeast and we will get zebrafish
in their too.

00:16:48.020 --> 00:16:49.260
C. elegans would be good too.

00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:51.620
Let's put that in there too.

00:16:51.620 --> 00:16:58.020
So, we have six selected sequences, and just
like shopping online I can add to my cart

00:16:58.020 --> 00:17:03.770
and get the six items.

00:17:03.770 --> 00:17:06.709
Now, suppose I want to compare the sequences?

00:17:06.709 --> 00:17:13.410
I can quickly do this by clicking this protein
alignment button.

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:16.980
And notice that I have the option of doing
the one sequence per gene thing.

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:19.890
It is pretty useful, especially if you're
going to do a protein alignment, you don't

00:17:19.890 --> 00:17:22.890
want to see all of the different isoforms.

00:17:22.890 --> 00:17:29.670
I can just align these.

00:17:29.670 --> 00:17:39.360
So, we have a nice alignment viewer here.

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:41.460
We also can look at the alignment down here.

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:46.190
This is a true multiple sequence alignment
using our multiple alignment tool Cobalt.

00:17:46.190 --> 00:17:51.650
And every residue of every protein is included
in this.

00:17:51.650 --> 00:17:57.520
The viewer up here is interesting, because
it has some features associated with the protein,

00:17:57.520 --> 00:18:03.360
so we can see for example this feature here
represents the active site.

00:18:03.360 --> 00:18:06.970
So, this is the active site of the protein.

00:18:06.970 --> 00:18:14.780
So, what you might expect is that these regions
in the protein are highly conserved, so let's

00:18:14.780 --> 00:18:19.150
zoom in to see you if that is the case.

00:18:19.150 --> 00:18:24.730
I will go ahead and color this in a way that
makes it suggestive.

00:18:24.730 --> 00:18:31.520
And we notice this area right here that is
the active site.

00:18:31.520 --> 00:18:35.850
These are conserved across all of these different
organisms.

00:18:35.850 --> 00:18:38.050
Okay.

00:18:38.050 --> 00:18:42.929
Let's go back and suppose we want to do some
other work with these sequences.

00:18:42.929 --> 00:18:48.680
And, let's go ahead and remove these from
the cart and I will go back.

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:54.250
Suppose I want to work with some nucleotide
sequences and want to do a BLAST search with

00:18:54.250 --> 00:18:55.250
them.

00:18:55.250 --> 00:19:00.100
I want to get some very closely related organisms.

00:19:00.100 --> 00:19:02.870
Let's pick a few mammals here.

00:19:02.870 --> 00:19:12.740
Human, mouse, rat, dog, and cow.

00:19:12.740 --> 00:19:16.030
And chimpanzee.

00:19:16.030 --> 00:19:19.620
So, my cart is empty and I can add these to
my cart.

00:19:19.620 --> 00:19:21.010
I have six items.

00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:25.370
Now, what I can do is download these.

00:19:25.370 --> 00:19:30.840
I can get the sequences themselves if I want,
but I can also get the accession numbers by

00:19:30.840 --> 00:19:33.700
during this tabular data here.

00:19:33.700 --> 00:19:46.570
I will go ahead and open that spreadsheet.

00:19:46.570 --> 00:19:49.960
Notice that we have some of those gene model
sequences in here.

00:19:49.960 --> 00:19:53.050
I will go ahead and collect all of those.

00:19:53.050 --> 00:19:58.830
So, I have copied them to my clipboard.

00:19:58.830 --> 00:20:12.490
Now, what I want to do is go to BLAST so we
can take advantage of the search experience.

00:20:12.490 --> 00:20:22.260
We can go to the BLAST page this way and let's
go to blastn. and here I have already set

00:20:22.260 --> 00:20:23.400
mine up recently.

00:20:23.400 --> 00:20:25.740
It remembers what I do sometimes.

00:20:25.740 --> 00:20:32.580
So, you can expand or contract this so you
can have a database search, or you can compare

00:20:32.580 --> 00:20:33.580
sets of sequences.

00:20:33.580 --> 00:20:38.529
So, I can just paste my sequences in here.

00:20:38.529 --> 00:20:44.160
What I want to do is to take the top sequence,
the human sequence and compare it to the other

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:48.720
sequences.

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:57.809
And, since I know that these are not very
closely related in terms of the nucleotide

00:20:57.809 --> 00:21:00.520
level, I can change the algorithm to BLASTN.

00:21:00.520 --> 00:21:04.490
If you don't do that, I'll leave that as an
exercise so you can see some of them won't

00:21:04.490 --> 00:21:05.490
align.

00:21:05.490 --> 00:21:06.490
And, I can run this search

00:21:06.490 --> 00:21:15.840
So this is making alignment not with the proteins,
but with the nucleotide sequences.

00:21:15.840 --> 00:21:19.760
And it is also BLAST and not a true multiple
sequence alignment.

00:21:19.760 --> 00:21:25.160
If we look at the alignments over here and
we can do this in a way that makes it interesting

00:21:25.160 --> 00:21:29.810
by changing this to flat query anchored with
dots.

00:21:29.810 --> 00:21:34.790
It sort of resembles the multiple alignment,
but it isn't because it doesn't include all

00:21:34.790 --> 00:21:35.790
of the residues.

00:21:35.790 --> 00:21:39.410
It is a local alignment, which BLAST is a
local alignment tool.

00:21:39.410 --> 00:21:44.130
For that reason, BLAST can give you some statistics
about how important the alignments you find

00:21:44.130 --> 00:21:46.380
are.

00:21:46.380 --> 00:21:48.530
That is one thing you can do with BLAST.

00:21:48.530 --> 00:21:49.700
Okay.

00:21:49.700 --> 00:21:56.730
Now, the last couple things I want to do,
is I want to do a regular BLAST search.

00:21:56.730 --> 00:22:02.900
We are going to get back to this idea of the
fact that this sequence is present in bacteria

00:22:02.900 --> 00:22:04.350
or some related sequence.

00:22:04.350 --> 00:22:12.120
Let me go back over here to my genes page
and I can go ahead and get the human protein

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:13.309
back.

00:22:13.309 --> 00:22:20.860
I know that this is the canonical RefSeqSelect.

00:22:20.860 --> 00:22:22.910
I can quickly get to the structure here.

00:22:22.910 --> 00:22:28.210
Another thing I can do quite easily is run
a BLAST search with this.

00:22:28.210 --> 00:22:30.290
Here is the protein BLAST page.

00:22:30.290 --> 00:22:34.790
I have already set it up so we can go to the
Model Organisms database.

00:22:34.790 --> 00:22:39.679
Which is a small database that contains several
completely sequenced genomes for representative

00:22:39.679 --> 00:22:40.890
organisms.

00:22:40.890 --> 00:22:43.809
It makes the search a lot faster and cleaner.

00:22:43.809 --> 00:22:48.240
If my only goal is to try to see if there
are matches in bacteria, this is a pretty

00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:50.250
good way to do it.

00:22:50.250 --> 00:22:52.670
Just a plug for BLAST.

00:22:52.670 --> 00:22:56.700
The most important thing you can do with up
BLAST search is to pick the smallest database

00:22:56.700 --> 00:22:59.190
that is likely containing the sequence of
interest.

00:22:59.190 --> 00:23:02.961
One way to do that is to use a taxonomy limit.

00:23:02.961 --> 00:23:09.270
We will limit it to bacteria.

00:23:09.270 --> 00:23:17.980
And this is a small database, so this shouldn't
take too long.

00:23:17.980 --> 00:23:20.320
And I have a lot of matches to bacteria.

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:25.280
Now, we talked a little bit about significance
in BLAST results.

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:31.420
The statistic that tells you the number of
matches we would expect by chance with the

00:23:31.420 --> 00:23:33.170
score or a better score.

00:23:33.170 --> 00:23:36.750
So, we can read this first one, for Synechocystis.

00:23:36.750 --> 00:23:40.480
That's 6 times 10 to the minus 47.

00:23:40.480 --> 00:23:41.530
That's no matches.

00:23:41.530 --> 00:23:43.799
I would expect no matches that are this good
by chance.

00:23:43.799 --> 00:23:48.340
If they are not due to chance, they are due
to something else, which is way back in the

00:23:48.340 --> 00:23:52.540
deeps of time, a common ancestor.

00:23:52.540 --> 00:23:58.120
At this end of the BLAST report, notice we
have E-values that are above 1.

00:23:58.120 --> 00:24:02.440
That means, I can't distinguish these results
from chance.

00:24:02.440 --> 00:24:04.270
That is the main thing that BLAST does for
you.

00:24:04.270 --> 00:24:09.330
It gives you something about the significance
of your matches.

00:24:09.330 --> 00:24:14.060
There is the canonical E. coli strain here,
K-12, and if you want to look at the alignment,

00:24:14.060 --> 00:24:15.200
you can do that here.

00:24:15.200 --> 00:24:20.860
This is how BLAST represents the alignment
and the identical residues are matched with

00:24:20.860 --> 00:24:26.830
the letter and if it is a conservative match,
you see the plus sign.

00:24:26.830 --> 00:24:35.490
The last task for today, I know we are close
on time.

00:24:35.490 --> 00:24:41.460
We should have a couple minutes, and we will
have some time at the end for questions.

00:24:41.460 --> 00:24:43.700
I am leaving this open until 12:45 for questions,
if you have any.

00:24:43.700 --> 00:24:50.830
So, we can go directly from this protein to
a structure, if we want to.

00:24:50.830 --> 00:24:54.660
That just takes us to the corresponding structures
that are similar.

00:24:54.660 --> 00:25:01.059
There was a study done with the human APRT
protein with other things bound to it, so

00:25:01.059 --> 00:25:02.300
they could look at the binding pockets.

00:25:02.300 --> 00:25:10.640
If we pick the first one, which has guanosine
monophosphate bound in the active site, in

00:25:10.640 --> 00:25:12.549
the binding pocket there.

00:25:12.549 --> 00:25:15.210
We have a little viewer that runs on the web
called iCn3D.

00:25:15.210 --> 00:25:23.299
I can simply load it directly . It brings
me up a viewer and we can see that this is

00:25:23.299 --> 00:25:31.260
highlighted, there are two protein chains
here and they are both APRT . And they have

00:25:31.260 --> 00:25:37.170
bound in the structures, this is the guanosine
monophosphate.

00:25:37.170 --> 00:25:45.220
And, you can see the alpha helices, and the
twisted beta sheet that forms the way this

00:25:45.220 --> 00:25:46.320
protein folds up.

00:25:46.320 --> 00:25:50.540
So, we have gone from the sequence up to the
folded up protein.

00:25:50.540 --> 00:25:53.750
You can even take a look at some of the functional
information on here if you want to.

00:25:53.750 --> 00:25:57.650
So let's add functional sites to this.

00:25:57.650 --> 00:26:04.190
There is an active site, the same one we saw
in the conservative domain on this protein

00:26:04.190 --> 00:26:05.440
a little while ago.

00:26:05.440 --> 00:26:08.419
I can highlight that by clicking on it.

00:26:08.419 --> 00:26:10.870
And these are the residues involved in the
active site.

00:26:10.870 --> 00:26:16.980
And, they form that sort of pocket surrounding
the GMP.

00:26:16.980 --> 00:26:22.830
And, you can take a look at that in the sequence,
if you want.

00:26:22.830 --> 00:26:30.790
And, in fact we can see that motif we saw
before as conserved here.

00:26:30.790 --> 00:26:33.950
That is the main thing that is highlighted
here.

00:26:33.950 --> 00:26:38.990
Okay, those are all of the sort of quick tour
things I wanted to show you.

00:26:38.990 --> 00:26:43.240
We talked about using the traditional search
mechanism, but I hope I pointed out to you

00:26:43.240 --> 00:26:48.770
that taking advantage of the new search is
a really good way to find the things you want.

00:26:48.770 --> 00:26:57.970
Let me put up the last slide, and while I
