00:29:10.470 --> 00:29:11.780
[Rana Morris] Okay.

00:29:11.780 --> 00:29:18.259
So, one of the users was a little confused
when you did your original search with APRT

00:29:18.259 --> 00:29:23.440
. You showed that they found a bacterial sequence
in that box.

00:29:23.440 --> 00:29:28.080
But, they got human sequences in that search
also.

00:29:28.080 --> 00:29:33.070
They want to know, why would you find human
sequences.

00:29:33.070 --> 00:29:40.130
And, as a second question, is this considered
data reuse or data discovery?

00:29:40.130 --> 00:29:42.330
If you wanted to put a term to this.

00:29:42.330 --> 00:29:48.480
[Peter Cooper] So, my search here is completely
unconstrained by organisms.

00:29:48.480 --> 00:29:54.250
If I do a search for adenine phosphoribosyltransferase,
I am just searching for that term indiscriminately

00:29:54.250 --> 00:29:58.039
across the data that are in the nucleotide
database.

00:29:58.039 --> 00:30:01.200
It is also the name of a protein.

00:30:01.200 --> 00:30:07.421
So, we thought it might be a useful result
for you to look in a protein database, which

00:30:07.421 --> 00:30:08.421
is what this is.

00:30:08.421 --> 00:30:14.380
It is a link to a protein adenine phosphoribosyltransferase
. That is a name for protein in bacteria.

00:30:14.380 --> 00:30:17.230
There is nothing particularly odd about that.

00:30:17.230 --> 00:30:22.610
We are assuming that you basically are searching
independent of the silos when we give you

00:30:22.610 --> 00:30:24.100
these boxes like this.

00:30:24.100 --> 00:30:26.570
So, if you do an all databases search.

00:30:26.570 --> 00:30:36.750
If I change this to all databases, notice
I get the same thing.

00:30:36.750 --> 00:30:44.350
And, that is because we know from what people
do when they search the NCBI databases, sometimes

00:30:44.350 --> 00:30:47.440
their queries make more sense in another database.

00:30:47.440 --> 00:30:51.669
And, because you are constrained to be in
that database, it makes it a little more challenging.

00:30:51.669 --> 00:30:55.000
But, if you have this it is more wide open.

00:30:55.000 --> 00:30:59.280
There is no reason why I wouldn't expect in
the Nuc database to get human sequences and

00:30:59.280 --> 00:31:02.020
bacterial sequences by searching for adenine
phoshoribosyltranserase.

00:31:02.020 --> 00:31:07.030
In fact, I did, but I just didn't have this
direct link to the protein.

00:31:07.030 --> 00:31:12.340
In fact, a lot of the bacterial matches in
my previous search were things like entire

00:31:12.340 --> 00:31:14.240
chromosomes of bacteria.

00:31:14.240 --> 00:31:17.190
Which shouldn't be terribly useful to you
if you were looking for this particular gene.

00:31:17.190 --> 00:31:22.669
I am not sure I quite understand the question
about data reuse or data discovery.

00:31:22.669 --> 00:31:24.870
This is essentially a search system.

00:31:24.870 --> 00:31:31.289
So, I suppose it would be classified as data
discovery, although I don't know if that particular

00:31:31.289 --> 00:31:33.330
term has a specific meaning.

00:31:33.330 --> 00:31:37.039
[Rana Morris] Another person going back to
the human Gene.

00:31:37.039 --> 00:31:41.850
When you do a search in Nuc you pull up all
sorts of sequences and you focused on the

00:31:41.850 --> 00:31:47.740
reference sequences, there are a number of
people that may be interested in ESTs or expressed

00:31:47.740 --> 00:31:48.740
sequence tags.

00:31:48.740 --> 00:31:52.750
Is there a way to narrow down those nucleotide
sequences for ESTs?

00:31:52.750 --> 00:31:55.340
[Peter Cooper] That is a good question.

00:31:55.340 --> 00:32:03.799
And in particular, it is a good question because
we have put the EST database back in, you

00:32:03.799 --> 00:32:06.750
may know the history of this.

00:32:06.750 --> 00:32:13.450
ESTs were old-fashioned technology, they were
separated out from the nucleotide database

00:32:13.450 --> 00:32:14.550
a long time ago.

00:32:14.550 --> 00:32:19.710
We just discontinued the EST database, so
they are back in there again.

00:32:19.710 --> 00:32:28.690
So, let's go back into here.

00:32:28.690 --> 00:32:29.690
And let's go nucleotide.

00:32:29.690 --> 00:32:32.150
And, let's just do a search for human.

00:32:32.150 --> 00:32:37.080
And actually, I haven't tried to separate
them out from nucleotide.

00:32:37.080 --> 00:32:39.760
It should be perfectly possible to do that.

00:32:39.760 --> 00:32:43.280
There are probably a number of ways to do
it.

00:32:43.280 --> 00:32:49.870
First I have to take my filter off.

00:32:49.870 --> 00:32:54.220
There is a filter on the leftt-hand side to
get ESTs.

00:32:54.220 --> 00:32:57.299
The other bulk category is GSS.

00:32:57.299 --> 00:33:10.530
If I click on the EST filter, then I have
8.7 million expressed sequence tags.

00:33:10.530 --> 00:33:16.880
And, if you want to know how you could write
that search in a formal way, that is how the

00:33:16.880 --> 00:33:19.789
EST filter looks if you write it out.

00:33:19.789 --> 00:33:21.910
I hope answered that question.

00:33:21.910 --> 00:33:22.910
Okay.

